Microorganisms, indicators and routes of exposure (@wu-microbes: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @water-and-sanitation, #source "../sources/Drinking-Water-Quality-WHO-2026.lingua#r_71BB45J2CZ76C0WZ6H1C7SWZ5F", #source "../sources/Biological-Wastewater-Treatment-Chen-and-Colleagues.lingua#r_1AE9DK0YWQR5GDECZ9KK1JJP37", #source "../sources/Water-Safety-Plan-Manual-WHO.lingua#r_3JMZKS3FG80FKX9YRQPEJMA4VD", #water-and-sanitation "../../../../stem/chemistry/general-chemistry/equilibrium/Mass-charge-and-speciation-balances.lingua#r_1PWQSP02CDTPRX1N5NPXTVJJAR") { r_6AFSE0XFBRE8Y27W0W57HGD017
**Read:** [Guidelines for drinking-water quality — WHO, 2026 consolidated edition](../sources/Drinking-Water-Quality-WHO-2026.lingua#r_71BB45J2CZ76C0WZ6H1C7SWZ5F), microbial aspects and the relevant organism fact sheets; [Biological Wastewater Treatment — Chen and colleagues, second edition](../sources/Biological-Wastewater-Treatment-Chen-and-Colleagues.lingua#r_1AE9DK0YWQR5GDECZ9KK1JJP37), chapter 2, microbiology and metabolism; [Water safety plan manual — WHO and IWA, second edition](../sources/Water-Safety-Plan-Manual-WHO.lingua#r_3JMZKS3FG80FKX9YRQPEJMA4VD), hazard assessment and verification.

Bacteria, viruses, protozoa and helminths differ in size, persistence, infectivity and treatment response. A treatment process that works well against one group need not provide the same control for another. Microorganisms also perform useful conversions in biological treatment; useful process activity and absence of harmful exposure are separate questions.

Trace a source of faecal contamination through transport, a possible exposure and the person or population concerned. Include ingestion and, where relevant, aerosols or contact. Distinguish a pathogen from an indicator organism, an indicator result from a complete risk assessment, and the presence of genetic material from demonstrated infectivity.

Read a laboratory result with its method, sample volume, detection capability and uncertainty. A nondetect in one sample is evidence bounded by that method and sampling event. Explain why a water service combines preventive controls, operational observations and independent verification rather than relying on one final sample.

